1
00:00:00,001 --> 00:00:07,000
Out of control, it's a monster with a voracious appetite.

2
00:00:07,001 --> 00:00:12,000
Properly harnessed, it's a force that has shaped our world.

3
00:00:12,001 --> 00:00:18,000
We've learned to create and exploit it, but will never truly tame it.

4
00:00:18,001 --> 00:00:25,000
From furnaces to flamethrowers, fire testing to fireballs.

5
00:00:25,001 --> 00:00:32,000
balls now fire on modern marbles

6
00:00:44,660 --> 00:00:51,560
late october 2003 wildfires devour the southern california landscape from san

7
00:00:51,561 --> 00:01:01,440
Diego to Ventura County, searing 750,000 acres of land, incinerating more than 3,600 homes,

8
00:01:01,441 --> 00:01:04,740
and killing 22 people.

9
00:01:04,741 --> 00:01:13,020
The Southern California wildfires of 2003 are the most devastating in the state's history.

10
00:01:13,021 --> 00:01:16,400
Wildfires are brutal reminders that fire is alive.

11
00:01:16,401 --> 00:01:21,100
It breathes, feeds, and grows.

12
00:01:21,101 --> 00:01:27,240
But while fire can be an all-consuming terror, it can also be our best friend.

13
00:01:27,241 --> 00:01:33,760
A raging fire is at the heart of one of the largest steam systems in the U.S.

14
00:01:33,761 --> 00:01:38,720
The TriGen Philadelphia steam plants generate steam heat for more than 500 buildings in

15
00:01:38,721 --> 00:01:42,940
downtown Philly.

16
00:01:42,941 --> 00:01:48,940
And in a city with winter temperatures falling well below freezing, the flow of heat is vital.

17
00:01:48,941 --> 00:01:56,580
The steam is delivered via 33 miles of underground pipe to customers throughout Center City and

18
00:01:56,581 --> 00:01:58,580
West Philadelphia.

19
00:01:58,581 --> 00:02:05,860
A billion pounds of steam coursing under the streets are the result of several massive

20
00:02:05,861 --> 00:02:07,860
fire-breathing boilers.

21
00:02:07,861 --> 00:02:12,420
We're standing on the burner floor of number 26 boiler.

22
00:02:12,421 --> 00:02:16,380
26 boiler is one of six boilers that we have here on site.

23
00:02:16,381 --> 00:02:21,260
This is actually an eight-story tall structure.

24
00:02:21,261 --> 00:02:25,700
The flame that you saw is consumed within six stories of that structure.

25
00:02:25,701 --> 00:02:30,440
So imagine if you will a six-story building that's completely involved or engulfed in

26
00:02:30,441 --> 00:02:33,140
flames contained inside this.

27
00:02:33,141 --> 00:02:38,780
A fire in an uncontrolled environment is a very dangerous thing.

28
00:02:38,781 --> 00:02:42,040
Forest fires, homes get burned down, things like that.

29
00:02:42,041 --> 00:02:47,200
What we're doing is we're taking that fire, that destructive property, channeling it for

30
00:02:47,201 --> 00:02:52,280
very constructive purposes and uses.

31
00:02:52,281 --> 00:02:58,400
The spectacular inferno generates temperatures of nearly 1500 degrees Fahrenheit, making

32
00:02:58,401 --> 00:03:01,160
short work of turning water into steam.

33
00:03:01,161 --> 00:03:05,580
So really what we're looking at here is the mother of all tea kettles.

34
00:03:05,581 --> 00:03:11,280
We're bringing water in, boiling it, sending it out to make steam for our customers.

35
00:03:11,281 --> 00:03:19,520
Trigen needs a gigantic fire in order to boil some 70,000 gallons of water each hour.

36
00:03:19,521 --> 00:03:21,940
And this fire needs to be fed.

37
00:03:21,941 --> 00:03:29,040
The number 26 boiler consumes number 6 oil, a less refined petroleum fuel.

38
00:03:29,041 --> 00:03:36,920
The Goliath fire can put away more than 160,000 gallons of fuel in just 24 hours.

39
00:03:36,921 --> 00:03:39,120
it needs to be closely monitored at all times.

40
00:03:41,080 --> 00:03:45,680
There are five people here dedicated on a 24-7 basis

41
00:03:45,681 --> 00:03:49,440
to the efficient, safe operations of these facilities.

42
00:03:50,340 --> 00:03:53,100
So they are highly skilled, highly trained people

43
00:03:53,101 --> 00:03:55,740
that are here to make sure that the fire doesn't die.

44
00:03:56,300 --> 00:03:59,880
Because without fire, bad things happen at that particular point in time.

45
00:04:01,180 --> 00:04:02,920
With sub-zero temperatures,

46
00:04:03,440 --> 00:04:06,520
Philadelphia has come to depend on the fire contained in these boilers.

47
00:04:06,920 --> 00:04:12,200
But our dependence on fire can at times be a terrible burden.

48
00:04:14,540 --> 00:04:17,440
We've developed a complex relationship with the flames.

49
00:04:18,500 --> 00:04:24,060
But what exactly is this incredibly powerful, strangely insubstantial thing we call fire?

50
00:04:24,740 --> 00:04:29,360
Fire is a very curious phenomenon because it's a reaction, not a substance.

51
00:04:29,620 --> 00:04:35,140
If you think of the other ancient elements, water, earth, air, they're all things, material substances.

52
00:04:35,141 --> 00:04:37,669
but fire is not. It's a reaction.

53
00:04:37,670 --> 00:04:41,960
During the reaction, molecules of fuel, primarily carbon

54
00:04:41,961 --> 00:04:46,161
and hydrogen, are reduced to smaller fragments by heat.

55
00:04:46,162 --> 00:04:49,140
Then these small fragments combine with

56
00:04:49,141 --> 00:04:54,692
oxygen, producing energy in the form of heat, perpetuating the cycle.

57
00:04:54,693 --> 00:04:56,060
Besides the heat,

58
00:04:56,400 --> 00:04:59,705
fire also releases energy in the form of light.

59
00:04:59,706 --> 00:05:03,080
The yellow and orange colors we see are actually

60
00:05:03,081 --> 00:05:05,180
produced by superheated carbon soot.

61
00:05:07,580 --> 00:05:11,760
And since the gases in the flame are hotter and less dense than the surrounding air,

62
00:05:12,200 --> 00:05:14,220
they move upward toward lower pressure.

63
00:05:17,440 --> 00:05:21,700
Fire, of course, has existed on Earth since long before the evolution of our species.

64
00:05:23,300 --> 00:05:24,980
Well, fire precedes people.

65
00:05:25,480 --> 00:05:29,560
I mean, it's been around on the planet for more than 400 million years.

66
00:05:29,680 --> 00:05:31,600
So it's something we didn't invent.

67
00:05:31,601 --> 00:05:32,637
We captured it.

68
00:05:32,638 --> 00:05:37,820
We gathered it from nature and then began remaking it to suit our purposes.

69
00:05:38,400 --> 00:05:40,740
It may be coming from the heavens in the form of lightning.

70
00:05:41,100 --> 00:05:46,640
I mean, you would have seen lightning struck trees, and that's where fire would be available in nature,

71
00:05:47,080 --> 00:05:48,840
and then you could collect it from there.

72
00:05:50,280 --> 00:05:53,740
Nearly every culture has a myth explaining the origin of fire.

73
00:05:56,800 --> 00:06:01,340
One of the most famous is the Greek myth, in which fire is illicitly gifted to man.

74
00:06:01,600 --> 00:06:07,600
Prometheus, who could think ahead, as his name suggests, has a brother Epimetheus, who

75
00:06:07,601 --> 00:06:09,600
can only think afterwards.

76
00:06:09,601 --> 00:06:15,600
And Epimetheus winds up giving all of the great traits to the animals and creation,

77
00:06:15,601 --> 00:06:20,600
and there's nothing left over by the time people come around, and so we're sort of,

78
00:06:20,601 --> 00:06:23,600
you know, pathetic creatures.

79
00:06:23,601 --> 00:06:30,600
And Prometheus then realizes that if we had fire, that relationship would change.

80
00:06:30,601 --> 00:06:35,060
Prometheus is said to have stolen fire from Zeus to give to man.

81
00:06:36,700 --> 00:06:42,600
As punishment, his liver was eaten by vultures, only to grow back and be eaten again.

82
00:06:44,220 --> 00:06:46,520
Zeus ordered this to go on for eternity.

83
00:06:46,860 --> 00:06:50,240
Because people in some sense then become like the gods.

84
00:06:50,420 --> 00:06:52,300
They share in that kind of power.

85
00:06:52,520 --> 00:06:53,320
Fire is power.

86
00:06:53,940 --> 00:06:56,680
Mythology aside, fire was a gift from the gods.

87
00:06:56,820 --> 00:06:59,920
Or at least the heavens, in the form of thunderbolts.

88
00:07:00,600 --> 00:07:08,960
It wasn't until 35,000 years ago that we began creating fire on our own.

89
00:07:10,820 --> 00:07:16,140
Fire-making technology quickly evolved with the creation of tools such as fire drills,

90
00:07:17,560 --> 00:07:19,180
bows, and plows.

91
00:07:20,100 --> 00:07:23,580
They all generate friction from rubbing two pieces of wood together.

92
00:07:24,540 --> 00:07:29,100
That friction creates heat, and eventually a spark that could ignite a fire.

93
00:07:30,600 --> 00:07:39,180
In the third century BC, ancient Greek mathematician and inventor Archimedes is said to have started

94
00:07:39,181 --> 00:07:42,920
fires by directing the sun's rays through a lens.

95
00:07:42,921 --> 00:07:51,840
But fire was more than just a tool in ancient Greece and Rome.

96
00:07:51,841 --> 00:07:58,860
Gods and goddesses of fire were worshipped and placed at the center of elaborate ceremonies.

97
00:07:58,861 --> 00:08:03,860
The Vestal Virgins of the Roman Empire had the sole ritual task of tending a flame in

98
00:08:03,861 --> 00:08:08,120
a community hearth.

99
00:08:08,121 --> 00:08:12,740
Their individual purity was considered essential to safeguarding the flame.

100
00:08:12,741 --> 00:08:16,980
And the penalty for breaking the vow of chastity was death.

101
00:08:16,981 --> 00:08:23,980
But fire also belonged to the underworld, where there was believed to be a river of fire,

102
00:08:23,981 --> 00:08:27,640
which was one of the rivers that separated the land of the living from that of the dead.

103
00:08:31,120 --> 00:08:36,220
Images of hellfire continued well past the 5th century AD and the fall of the Roman Empire.

104
00:08:38,780 --> 00:08:43,400
The aptly named Dark Ages produced few advancements in fire-making technology.

105
00:08:46,200 --> 00:08:53,520
Finally, the 16th century saw the invention of a portable fire-making kit called the tinderbox.

106
00:08:53,980 --> 00:08:58,620
The tinderbox consisted of three parts.

107
00:08:59,640 --> 00:09:01,160
Flint and steel to make a spark.

108
00:09:02,040 --> 00:09:02,940
Tinder to ignite.

109
00:09:03,940 --> 00:09:05,900
And a candle to hold the flame.

110
00:09:07,340 --> 00:09:09,660
When you put those three together, you get fire.

111
00:09:10,280 --> 00:09:11,580
And then you can add to it.

112
00:09:12,020 --> 00:09:14,540
But you're going to have to carry all that with you.

113
00:09:15,020 --> 00:09:18,260
So your toolkit for fire can be fairly complicated.

114
00:09:19,480 --> 00:09:23,140
But that all changed in 1827 with one invention.

115
00:09:23,980 --> 00:09:25,140
The friction match.

116
00:09:27,020 --> 00:09:31,560
The Diamond Match brand has been producing matches for more than 130 years.

117
00:09:32,960 --> 00:09:36,000
Today, they produce nearly 10 billion matches annually.

118
00:09:37,040 --> 00:09:39,820
And half of those are in the form of kitchen matches.

119
00:09:42,380 --> 00:09:44,620
The process starts with logs.

120
00:09:47,700 --> 00:09:49,320
Which are debarked.

121
00:09:50,720 --> 00:09:53,060
Cut into 25-inch long blocks.

122
00:09:53,980 --> 00:09:56,900
then shaved into sheets an eighth of an inch thick.

123
00:09:58,780 --> 00:10:01,660
The sheets are chopped into sticks called splints.

124
00:10:03,860 --> 00:10:08,640
These bare matchsticks are then dipped into a brine tank of monoammonium phosphate.

125
00:10:10,960 --> 00:10:17,420
Many years ago, Diamond pioneered that process, and it's really the connotation of safety match.

126
00:10:18,320 --> 00:10:22,420
The monoammonium phosphate, once you blow out the match, it stops the afterglow,

127
00:10:22,421 --> 00:10:26,420
which is the safety feature so it doesn't continue to glow and cause a fire.

128
00:10:27,500 --> 00:10:30,860
But this wasn't the only safety feature of diamond brand matches.

129
00:10:32,300 --> 00:10:35,980
Early 19th century matches, or lucifers as they were nicknamed,

130
00:10:36,480 --> 00:10:39,700
were generally made of volatile and poisonous white phosphorus.

131
00:10:41,560 --> 00:10:44,020
They made it almost too easy to start fire.

132
00:10:45,240 --> 00:10:48,120
Simply dropping or stepping on the matches would often ignite them.

133
00:10:48,121 --> 00:11:00,504
And if that wasn't bad enough, match plant workers breathing in high levels of toxic phosphorus fumes often suffered from necrosis, or Fosse Jaw, a condition that

134
00:11:00,505 --> 00:11:05,320
infected and decayed the mouth and jaw, and was at times fatal.

135
00:11:08,020 --> 00:11:14,820
But by 1911, chemists at Diamond Brand Matches formulated the first non-poisonous Strike Anywhere match.

136
00:11:18,120 --> 00:11:22,800
A similar formula is used today.

137
00:11:22,801 --> 00:11:27,500
After the splints are dried, polished, and placed in slots, they're ready to get their

138
00:11:27,501 --> 00:11:28,500
tips.

139
00:11:28,501 --> 00:11:34,140
Once they're placed in the chain, the first step is to dip it in wax.

140
00:11:34,141 --> 00:11:37,240
The wax is the transmitter between the flame and the wood.

141
00:11:37,241 --> 00:11:41,840
Without the wax, the flame wouldn't transfer and the match wouldn't continue to burn.

142
00:11:41,841 --> 00:11:45,800
It would just go right out.

143
00:11:45,801 --> 00:11:49,080
The next step is where the head is actually put on.

144
00:11:49,081 --> 00:11:50,960
We call this composition.

145
00:11:50,961 --> 00:11:53,660
It's made up of several different chemicals.

146
00:11:55,360 --> 00:11:57,100
The composition is dyed red

147
00:11:57,101 --> 00:11:59,880
because that's the way Diamond Brand likes it.

148
00:11:59,881 --> 00:12:01,820
But the tips can be virtually any color.

149
00:12:03,460 --> 00:12:05,180
Matches are made up of approximately

150
00:12:05,181 --> 00:12:07,280
20 different types of chemicals.

151
00:12:07,281 --> 00:12:12,280
Predominantly it's phosphorus, potassium fluorate, and sulfur.

152
00:12:12,480 --> 00:12:15,040
And it's the reaction between those three main ingredients

153
00:12:15,041 --> 00:12:18,340
that starts a fire.

154
00:12:18,341 --> 00:12:21,120
There are about one million matches on the match machine

155
00:12:21,121 --> 00:12:22,960
at any given time.

156
00:12:22,961 --> 00:12:26,060
And at the end of a typical shift, more than 11 million

157
00:12:26,061 --> 00:12:28,420
matches have been produced.

158
00:12:28,421 --> 00:12:31,060
If you combine all the matches on the machines

159
00:12:31,061 --> 00:12:34,760
for one year that we manufacture over a period of one year,

160
00:12:34,761 --> 00:12:37,120
you can light them up end to end and they go around the Earth

161
00:12:37,121 --> 00:12:38,920
at the equator about 12 times.

162
00:12:38,921 --> 00:12:46,920
The match put fire in our cupboards and in our pockets.

163
00:12:46,921 --> 00:12:54,740
Now we were ready to harness its power, to propel us across a continent and into outer

164
00:12:54,741 --> 00:13:01,020
space.

165
00:13:01,021 --> 00:13:06,560
The word match comes from the name of a dried fungus, Mykies, that the ancient Greeks ignited

166
00:13:06,561 --> 00:13:08,780
with sparks from a flint.

167
00:13:08,781 --> 00:13:13,340
Fire will return on Modern Marvels here on the History Channel.

168
00:13:15,420 --> 00:13:18,740
We now return to fire on Modern Marvels.

169
00:13:22,820 --> 00:13:28,220
The concentrated power of fire is rarely more evident than on the launching pad.

170
00:13:29,580 --> 00:13:34,820
Seven million pounds of flaming thrust have propelled humanity to new frontiers.

171
00:13:34,821 --> 00:13:36,820
I'll turn it low enough.

172
00:13:36,821 --> 00:13:45,180
By harnessing the energy of combustion and incorporating the Newtonian principle that every action has an equal and opposite reaction,

173
00:13:46,340 --> 00:13:49,120
we've repeatedly launched ourselves into orbit.

174
00:13:51,260 --> 00:13:57,660
Perhaps no other feat illustrates our degree of mastery over one of the fiercest sources of energy in the solar system.

175
00:13:58,620 --> 00:14:03,800
In a sense, it is a captured element, ecological process that we have captured and trained,

176
00:14:03,920 --> 00:14:06,240
the way we might train a grizzly bear to dance.

177
00:14:06,820 --> 00:14:12,980
And we can use it in the wide world out there, but always with the understanding that it could go feral.

178
00:14:14,560 --> 00:14:18,080
Indeed, sometimes fire throws off the shackles of technology.

179
00:14:26,400 --> 00:14:29,320
And reasserts itself with a demonic rage.

180
00:14:36,820 --> 00:14:45,500
The fire-fueled revolution in travel began more than 150 years ago, when we used fire

181
00:14:45,501 --> 00:14:49,820
to power us across vast landscapes with the mighty steam locomotive.

182
00:14:49,821 --> 00:14:59,320
The Maryland Scenic Railroad's 1916 Baldwin 280, nicknamed Mountain Thunder, at one time

183
00:14:59,321 --> 00:15:05,860
hauled iron ore in Michigan's Upper Peninsula.

184
00:15:05,861 --> 00:15:13,400
Today it transports a much lighter load, passengers, but that's still a total of 1,200 tons it

185
00:15:13,401 --> 00:15:18,740
needs to muscle on a 16-mile scenic journey.

186
00:15:18,741 --> 00:15:24,460
And the seat of its strength and generative power can be found deep inside its boiler.

187
00:15:24,461 --> 00:15:26,740
Fire is what creates the steam.

188
00:15:26,741 --> 00:15:32,740
Without a good fire, you will have no power, and with no power you can't move.

189
00:15:32,741 --> 00:15:35,500
So essentially, fire is everything.

190
00:15:37,140 --> 00:15:40,160
The engine room is the burning core of the locomotive.

191
00:15:42,200 --> 00:15:46,560
Here, the fireman tends to the fire that creates the steam that powers the train.

192
00:15:47,740 --> 00:15:51,640
Fire is as every bit as a living thing as you can get.

193
00:15:52,220 --> 00:15:55,120
You've got to nurture it. You've got to take care of it.

194
00:15:55,900 --> 00:16:02,040
If you treat it wrong, it'll bite you. It'll grow. It'll die.

195
00:16:02,740 --> 00:16:06,060
But Rick won't let that happen.

196
00:16:06,660 --> 00:16:09,860
Right now, this fire is nearly 3,000 degrees Fahrenheit

197
00:16:09,861 --> 00:16:14,580
and needs to devour more than two tons of coal just to make it to the top of the hill.

198
00:16:16,280 --> 00:16:19,140
But steam locomotives didn't always consume coal.

199
00:16:19,820 --> 00:16:24,960
First engines were wood-burning engines with very low steam pressure.

200
00:16:25,320 --> 00:16:30,960
They needed a higher rate of pressure to create bigger locomotives to make more horsepower.

201
00:16:30,961 --> 00:16:34,920
They needed more heat, and more heat comes from the coals.

202
00:16:37,320 --> 00:16:40,560
Coal consists of nearly double the carbon found in wood,

203
00:16:41,220 --> 00:16:43,600
allowing it to produce nearly three times the heat.

204
00:16:46,260 --> 00:16:51,780
Fire is complicated, and the chemistry of even a single candle flame is a very complex phenomenon.

205
00:16:52,480 --> 00:16:56,840
All the chemistry, the chemistry of combustion, happens where the blue is,

206
00:16:56,841 --> 00:17:02,320
and because this is a burning of solid fuel, it has a tendency to produce a lot of soot.

207
00:17:02,820 --> 00:17:06,280
So the yellow you see here is soot that forms from the wax

208
00:17:06,281 --> 00:17:12,480
and again these are solid particles that when they find themselves in an environment that is very hot,

209
00:17:12,640 --> 00:17:13,540
they emit yellow light.

210
00:17:14,300 --> 00:17:17,320
But in a zero-gravity environment, like space,

211
00:17:17,520 --> 00:17:21,880
the flame behaves differently and manifests itself as a blue sphere.

212
00:17:21,881 --> 00:17:27,220
Trapping all the soot inside, this sphere cuts off the oxygen supply.

213
00:17:28,120 --> 00:17:31,440
So in space, a candle flame won't last more than a minute.

214
00:17:32,720 --> 00:17:37,280
But even on Earth, flames from refined fossil fuels such as natural gas

215
00:17:37,281 --> 00:17:39,640
produce a different-looking flame than the candle.

216
00:17:42,800 --> 00:17:46,100
An interesting phenomenon here is the color of the flame.

217
00:17:46,101 --> 00:17:48,700
By adding more fuel, which is natural gas,

218
00:17:48,701 --> 00:17:52,420
we see that the color was converted to green from blue.

219
00:17:52,880 --> 00:17:56,400
What we have now here is a hydrogen flame, pure hydrogen flame.

220
00:17:56,780 --> 00:17:59,580
So we mix hydrogen and air, and we have a flame.

221
00:17:59,860 --> 00:18:03,160
However, there is nothing that the naked eye can observe.

222
00:18:03,600 --> 00:18:08,040
The reason that we cannot see anything is that hydrogen does not contain carbon.

223
00:18:08,320 --> 00:18:09,920
However, a flame does exist.

224
00:18:10,540 --> 00:18:15,320
And to demonstrate that, I will put a piece of paper, and as you see, it ignites.

225
00:18:15,321 --> 00:18:21,140
hydrogen fuel is environmentally friendly because its only byproduct is water

226
00:18:21,141 --> 00:18:26,040
while it's very efficient to burn it's still very costly to produce

227
00:18:26,041 --> 00:18:34,380
hydrogen is considered to be the fuel of the century and of course we'll be able to use it

228
00:18:34,381 --> 00:18:39,100
if we manage to overcome the technical difficulties of production and the economical aspects of the

229
00:18:39,101 --> 00:18:46,320
In the meantime, other green fuels are coming, or rather coming back into the picture.

230
00:18:47,560 --> 00:18:56,020
At GWF Power Systems' electric generation plant in Tracy, California, the fuel of choice is biomass.

231
00:18:59,460 --> 00:19:05,360
Biomass is organic matter, generally diseased trees and underbrush, that can be used as fuel.

