00:19:56,681 --> 00:20:03,000
Wood chips are used instead of larger blocks of wood because they offer a greater surface

242
00:20:03,001 --> 00:20:07,440
area, allowing them to burn more quickly and produce more heat.

243
00:20:07,441 --> 00:20:16,000
The temperatures range from 1400 to 2000 degrees depending on what type of wood is on the grate

244
00:20:16,001 --> 00:20:17,000
at the time.

245
00:20:17,001 --> 00:20:23,000
The 16 foot tall firebox is 24 feet deep and 16 feet wide.

246
00:20:23,001 --> 00:20:32,000
The flames are four to six feet tall, also depending on how much air and how much wood is in there at any given time.

247
00:20:33,460 --> 00:20:38,980
Unlike burning gasoline or natural gas, burning wood occurs in two stages.

248
00:20:40,060 --> 00:20:45,760
First, volatile gases released from the wood ignite and fire starts, just as with fossil fuel.

249
00:20:45,761 --> 00:20:53,520
But then the carbon in the char combines with oxygen, and a slower combustion reaction occurs as well.

250
00:20:54,420 --> 00:21:01,680
Class S starts here with the fire. We basically turn in fire into steam.

251
00:21:03,560 --> 00:21:11,320
The steam is used to drive a turbine that generates enough electricity to power over 20,000 homes each day.

252
00:21:11,321 --> 00:21:16,440
But sometimes fire hits too close to home.

253
00:21:17,440 --> 00:21:21,520
And when it does, technology and testing have us prepared.

