00:24:59,841 --> 00:25:06,840
And raged out of control in San Francisco after the great earthquake of 1906.

303
00:25:06,841 --> 00:25:15,840
And only at those times did we decide to forego constructing our urban landscape in fuel-rich timber in favor of fired brick.

304
00:25:15,841 --> 00:25:18,880
Fires were always good for the brick industry.

305
00:25:19,680 --> 00:25:26,140
We had the Great London Fire of 1666, which caused a tremendous amount of brick construction in that city.

306
00:25:26,141 --> 00:25:32,060
Its usefulness in terms of fire resistance is one of the most principal reasons for its success as a building material.

307
00:25:33,600 --> 00:25:37,720
And fire has been used to modify a variety of other materials, including metal.

308
00:25:39,540 --> 00:25:45,800
The steel that frames our cities wouldn't exist if iron hadn't first been put through the crucible of the blast furnace.

309
00:25:45,840 --> 00:25:54,560
An amazing amount of the world around us has to undergo a baptism by fire at some point in the production process

310
00:25:54,561 --> 00:25:58,740
We can build because of our mastery of fire

311
00:25:58,741 --> 00:26:01,160
Yet we also build in fear of it

312
00:26:01,161 --> 00:26:04,120
And as much as we try to fireproof our world

313
00:26:04,121 --> 00:26:06,800
Fire can find fuel almost anywhere

314
00:26:06,801 --> 00:26:10,980
In our homes, from the Christmas tree to the couch

315
00:26:10,981 --> 00:26:12,880
The carpet to the curtains

316
00:26:12,881 --> 00:26:16,300
They're all fodder for flames if given half a chance

