00:30:09,280 --> 00:30:13,340
But the Molotov cocktail certainly wasn't the first use of fire in warfare.

367
00:30:13,900 --> 00:30:18,660
There are fire arrows and fire spears.

368
00:30:18,661 --> 00:30:23,480
There are attempts to burn fortresses if they're made out of combustible materials,

369
00:30:24,040 --> 00:30:28,060
burn cities if they have combustible roofs, which most ancient cities would.

370
00:30:28,740 --> 00:30:34,260
In the 7th century AD, civilization's most vicious use of flames was Greek fire,

371
00:30:34,261 --> 00:30:38,760
a flammable liquid that stuck to whatever it hit and could not be extinguished with water.

372
00:30:39,520 --> 00:30:43,500
This was a terrifying device, and Greek fire could be widely used,

373
00:30:43,600 --> 00:30:47,480
particularly against, say, naval vessels that were combustible because they were wood.

374
00:30:48,360 --> 00:30:51,980
The secret formula for this savage fire survived for centuries.

375
00:30:53,220 --> 00:30:57,820
As long as we were still using projector weapons such as arrows and using spears and swords

